ITEMS=[]
def Q(stem,key,d1,d2,d3,why):ITEMS.append((stem,[key,d1,d2,d3],why))
Q("An ordinary licensed firearm will be left at home while its owner is away, and nobody has lawful direct control of it. What does the law require?",
 "Keep it in a prescribed safe or strongroom","Hide it out of sight in a locked cupboard","Leave it with a neighbour without paperwork","Keep it unloaded in its holster",
 "Regulation 86 requires storage in a prescribed safe or strongroom when the firearm is not under direct personal and physical control, unless a specific exception applies.")
Q("A lawfully carried handgun is in an appropriate holder and remains controlled, but part of it can be seen in a public place. Which section 84 requirement is not satisfied?",
 "It must be completely covered","It must be unloaded","It must be held in the hand","It must have a trigger lock",
 "Section 84 requires the firearm to be completely covered as well as carried in an appropriate holder under effective control.")
Q("A handgun is covered in an appropriate holder in a bag, but the owner leaves the bag on a cafe table while walking to the counter. Which carrying requirement is missing?",
 "Effective control over the firearm","A locking holster","A copy of the licence in the bag","A completely covered holder",
 "Covering and a suitable holder do not replace the separate duty to keep the firearm under effective control.")
Q("A firearm is properly locked away, but the owner leaves the key on the kitchen counter where visitors could take it. Which separate obligation has been neglected?",
 "Keeping the keys secure","Changing the safe's lock every year","Registering the key with the DFO","Keeping a spare key in the car",
 "Section 120(8) deals with keeping the safe keys in safe custody, as well as locking the firearm away.")
Q("A proposed regulation 86(4) storage authorisation identifies the people, the firearm and the reason, but does not say from when or for how long. What required information is missing?",
 "The period of the storage","The serial number of the safe","The storage fee agreed","The firearm's date of purchase",
 "The written authorisation must specify the period for which storage is allowed.")
Q("A storage authorisation gives dates and identifies the parties and the firearm but never says why the temporary arrangement is needed. What required information is missing?",
 "The reason for the arrangement","The storage provider's employer","The insurer's details","The value of the firearm",
 "Regulation 86(4)(c) requires the reason for storage as well as its period and the identifying details.")
Q("A storage document describes the firearm and gives the dates but leaves out the storage provider's personal particulars. What is incomplete?",
 "Their name, ID number and address","Their bank details and tax number","Their medical history and doctor","Their employer's name and contact",
 "The authorisation must identify both people concerned by name, identity number and physical address.")
Q("A storage document names both people and gives a reason and period, but only says \"one firearm\". What required particulars are missing?",
 "Details of the firearm and its licence","The firearm's current market value","The brand of ammunition it uses","The year and country it was made",
 "The document must sufficiently identify the firearm and the licence, permit or authorisation concerned.")
Q("A licence holder wants another licensed person to store a firearm under regulation 86(4), but the document was drawn up by a neighbour acting without the holder's say-so. Which requirement is missing?",
 "Written authorisation from the lawful holder","Written consent from the neighbour's spouse","A written valuation of the firearm","A written statement from a dealer",
 "The written authorisation must come from the person legally entitled to possess the firearm.")
Q("Both parties have signed an otherwise complete regulation 86(4) storage arrangement, but no police official has signed it off. What approval is still needed?",
 "The DFO's endorsement","A commissioner of oaths' stamp","A dealer's countersignature","A neighbour's signature as witness",
 "The holder's written authorisation must be endorsed by the relevant Designated Firearms Officer.")
Q("An endorsed storage authorisation names one address, but the storage provider proposes keeping the firearm at a different address. Which requirement would be breached?",
 "A prescribed safe at the named address","Storage at any address the provider owns","Storage at the holder's own address only","Notice of the new address within 30 days",
 "The firearm must be in a prescribed safe at the place specified in the authorisation.")
Q("A person with no firearm licence and no relevant muzzle-loading competency offers to store a firearm under regulation 86(4), relying on written consent alone. Which threshold requirement is absent?",
 "Qualifying status of the provider themselves","The holder's competency certificate","A separate licence issued for the safe","A second competency test for the holder",
 "The storage provider must have the qualifying status set out in regulation 86(4), in addition to the endorsed written authority and a proper safe.")
Q("A person authorised only to store another owner's firearm asks to take it for personal practice during the storage period. What does regulation 86(4) say?",
 "It may not be used while so stored","It may be used on licensed ranges only","It may be used with the owner's consent","It may be used for self-defence in emergencies",
 "A firearm stored under that provision may not be used by the storage provider or anyone else while it is so stored.")
Q("An uninvolved friend offers to take a firearm out of a regulation 86(4) safe for the owner. Who may remove it?",
 "Only its holder or someone permitted","Any adult the storage provider trusts","Any licensed firearm owner nearby","Any member of the provider's household",
 "Removal is limited to the lawful holder or the person granted the specified permission. A friend is not automatically authorised.")
Q("An endorsed storage arrangement lets a qualifying person move a firearm to and from the named safe on stated conditions. What governs that movement?",
 "Only what the Registrar or DFO authorised","Any trip, if the firearm is unloaded","Any trip within the same suburb","Free movement, as storage is endorsed",
 "The 2012 amendment allows the relevant authorised person to transport the firearm only as authorised by the Registrar or DFO.")
Q("A DFO identifies good cause why a temporary-storage arrangement should not continue. What power does regulation 86(4)(d) give?",
 "Cancel the permission at any time","Only suspend it for 30 days","Refer it to the Appeal Board first","Cancel it only when its period ends",
 "The relevant DFO may cancel the storage permission at any time on good cause.")
Q("For ordinary home storage when a licensed firearm is not under personal control, what is the default facility required by regulation 86(1)?",
 "A SABS-standard safe or strongroom","A locked steel cupboard of any make","A locked vehicle boot at home","A lockable gun case under a bed",
 "The default is a safe or strongroom that conforms to the referenced SABS standards, subject to specific regulatory provisions.")
Q("An owner relies on an existing safe approved under the previous law. What formal recognition is required for the deemed-compliance provision, rather than assuming its age is enough?",
 "A DFO certificate for that safe","Proof the safe is over ten years old","A dealer's receipt for the safe","Nothing, as old safes are exempt",
 "Under regulation 86(8), as amended, the DFO must issue the relevant certificate for a qualifying existing safe.")
Q("At premises with a lawful firearm-control arrangement, the alternative locker opens only when two specific people act together. Which option is described?",
 "A regulation 86(5) lock-away safe","A strongroom built to the SABS standard","A temporary storage authorisation","A dealer's secured storeroom",
 "Regulation 86(5) allows a prescribed installed lock-away safe needing joint opening, alongside the compliant safe or strongroom option.")
Q("An owner says the storage rule has not been triggered because the firearm is on their hip at all times. Which factual question decides that claim?",
 "Whether it is under their direct control","Whether it is in the same house as them","Whether the outside doors are locked","Whether it is unloaded in the holster",
 "The regulation contrasts prescribed storage with direct personal and physical control. Being somewhere in the house is not the same thing.")
Q("An owner puts all ammunition in the compliant safe but leaves the firearm unattended in an ordinary drawer, with no exception applying. Which item still needs the prescribed custody?",
 "The firearm","The licence card","The holster","Nothing else, since the ammunition is locked",
 "Securing the ammunition alone does not satisfy the storage duty for the unattended firearm.")
Q("A firearm is correctly locked away, but a box of cartridges is left on a shelf outside the safe, with no exception applying. What remains improperly stored?",
 "The ammunition","The cleaning kit","The licence card","Nothing further",
 "Regulation 86(1) applies to the firearm and its ammunition.")
Q("An owner's firearms and ammunition are secure, but the licence card is allowed to become unreadable through damage. Which document-related duty remains unsatisfied?",
 "Keeping the licence legible","Carrying the licence at all times","Keeping a photocopy of the licence","Laminating the licence card",
 "Section 106 requires the licence, permit or authorisation to be kept in an undefaced and legible condition.")
Q("A compliant safe holds the firearm and ammunition, but its key is handed to an unauthorised person. Which part of access control must be corrected?",
 "Safe custody of the key","Bolting the safe to the wall","Counting the ammunition","Cleaning the firearm",
 "Safe storage includes safe custody of the key. A compliant safe is not enough if access is uncontrolled.")
Q("A person lawfully carries a handgun in public. Which requirement concerns the kind of holster, rucksack or similar carrier used, rather than visibility or control?",
 "A suitable holder for a handgun","Carrying the handgun unloaded","Carrying it in plain view at all times","Fitting a trigger lock to it",
 "Section 84(1)(a) sets the permitted type of handgun holder, including an appropriate holster attached to the body or a rucksack or similar holder.")
Q("A person lawfully carries a firearm other than a handgun in public. Which carrier requirement does section 84(1)(b) set?",
 "A holder made or adapted for it","Any bag large enough to hold it","A handgun-style holster on the belt","No holder at all if it is unloaded",
 "It must be in a holder designed, manufactured or adapted for carrying that firearm, as well as covered and controlled.")
Q("An owner argues that an appropriate holster alone makes a visibly exposed firearm comply with section 84. Which further requirement deals with visibility?",
 "Complete covering of the firearm","Unloading the firearm","Using a locking holster","Nothing further, as the holster is enough",
 "The firearm must be completely covered. An appropriate holder does not remove that requirement.")
Q("A covered firearm in a suitable carrier is within sight but beyond the person's ability to reach or supervise it. Which section 84 requirement still needs attention?",
 "Effective control by the carrier","Keeping it within sight at all times","Keeping it covered in its holder","Keeping it unloaded while carried",
 "The person carrying the firearm must be able to exercise effective control. Being able to see it from a distance is not enough.")
Q("An ordinary section 15 licence holder, who is not a collector and has no other relevant exception, stores the firearm at home. What does regulation 86(11)(a) require about its loaded state?",
 "It must be stored unloaded","It may be stored loaded in the safe","It may be loaded if the safe is locked","Only the magazine must be removed",
 "Firearms outside the stated exceptions must be stored unloaded. This scenario expressly rules out the exceptions.")
Q("A lawful owner asks whether regulation 86(11)(a) imposes the same unloaded-storage rule on a firearm licensed for personal protection under section 13. Which provision matters?",
 "The section 13 exception in that regulation","The collector exception","An exception for dedicated sport shooters","None, as every firearm is stored unloaded",
 "The unloaded-storage provision expressly distinguishes section 13 firearms. That does not remove the other safe-custody obligations.")
Q("A qualifying collector considers keeping one loaded firearm in the safe for self-defence. Which part of the 2012 amendment is relevant?",
 "The collector's one-firearm exception","The section 13 self-defence exception","An exception for dedicated hunters","None, as collectors have no exception",
 "The amended regulation includes a collector exception for one loaded firearm in the safe for self-defence. Other custody requirements still apply.")
Q("An owner says only that a firearm is \"for protection\" but gives no licence category or collector status. Can an unloaded-storage exception be concluded from those words alone?",
 "No, the legal category must be known","Yes, any protection purpose is exempt","Yes, the owner's word is enough","No, firearms may never be stored loaded",
 "The legal category and conditions matter. A casual description of purpose does not establish a statutory exception.")
Q("An ordinary owner relies on section 90(a) to possess ammunition. What relationship must exist between the ammunition and the licensed firearm?",
 "The licensed firearm must be able to fire it","Any calibre works under any firearm licence","It must come from the dealer who sold the firearm","It must be fewer than 200 rounds",
 "The licensed firearm must be capable of discharging that ammunition. A licence for an unrelated firearm is not enough under that paragraph.")
Q("A person relies on a separate document issued specifically for ammunition, not on a firearm licence. Which basis in section 90 is being used?",
 "A separate ammunition permit","A firearm licence for that calibre","A competency certificate","A shop receipt for the cartridges",
 "Section 90(b) recognises a permit to possess ammunition as a distinct lawful basis.")
Q("A properly licensed dealer holds ammunition as part of its lawful business. Which basis in section 90 applies, apart from any personal firearm licence?",
 "Its dealer's licence","A personal firearm licence","An ammunition permit held by its owner","Its business registration certificate",
 "Section 90(c) includes dealers and other listed trade or transport authorities.")
Q("A person holds a valid authority under the Act that permits the ammunition possession but is not a licence for a compatible firearm or an ammunition permit. Which basis in section 90 applies?",
 "Being otherwise lawfully authorised","A personal belief that it is lawful","A shop receipt for the ammunition","A licence for an unrelated firearm",
 "Section 90(d) recognises a person who is otherwise lawfully authorised. A belief or a receipt is not such authority.")
Q("A licensed ordinary owner has no dedicated or professional status, no quantity authorisation and no range exception. What is the maximum number of cartridges for each licensed firearm?",
 "200 cartridges","100 cartridges","500 cartridges","No set limit",
 "Section 91(1) sets the ordinary ceiling at 200 cartridges for each licensed firearm, subject to the express exceptions.")
Q("A licensed dedicated sport shooter asks how the section 91 quantity limit applies to them. What is the position?",
 "Dedicated status exempts them from it","The limit is doubled to 400","The same 200 limit applies","They need a range officer's permission",
 "Section 91(2)(a) exempts licensed dedicated hunters, dedicated sport shooters and professional hunters from the ordinary ceiling.")
Q("An ordinary licence holder without dedicated status shows good cause for holding more than 200 cartridges for a licensed firearm. Which route is available?",
 "Asking the Registrar to authorise more","Buying from several different dealers","Getting a letter from a range officer","Storing the extra at a friend's house",
 "The Registrar may authorise a larger quantity on good cause. The person must obtain that authority, not simply exceed the limit.")
Q("A licence holder buys cartridges at an accredited shooting range and shoots them there. Which express exception covers that ammunition?",
 "Ammunition bought and fired at the range","Ammunition bought at a range and taken home","Ammunition reloaded at home","Ammunition received as a gift",
 "Section 91(2)(b) concerns ammunition bought and discharged at an accredited range, not an unlimited quantity taken home.")
Q("An ordinary owner has exactly 200 cartridges for a compatible licensed firearm and meets all other requirements. What is the position under the numerical ceiling alone?",
 "It is within the limit","It is over the limit","It needs the Registrar's approval","It is allowed only for dedicated holders",
 "The prohibition is on more than 200 cartridges. Exactly 200 does not exceed the ceiling.")
Q("An ordinary owner has 190 cartridges for a licensed firearm and plans to add a sealed box of 25, with no exemption or authorisation. What is the result?",
 "215 rounds, which is over the limit","215 rounds, which is within the limit","Allowed, since sealed boxes are not counted","Allowed, since the excess is under 20",
 "190 plus 25 is 215, which exceeds the ordinary 200-cartridge ceiling. Packaging does not change the count.")
Q("An owner has 20 rifle cartridges but is licensed only for a shotgun, and has no other authority. What is the legal issue despite the small quantity?",
 "No licensed firearm of theirs fires them","None, since the quantity is small","Only a question of storage location","None, as long as they are in the safe",
 "The quantity ceiling does not itself authorise possession. Section 90 requires a lawful basis, and under paragraph (a) the licensed firearm must be able to fire the ammunition.")
Q("An ordinary owner already holds the maximum quantity and buys more cartridges at an accredited range, intending to take them all home unused. Why is the range exception not enough?",
 "It covers only ammunition fired at the range","Range ammunition never counts toward the limit","It allows 200 extra rounds at home","Buying at a range needs no licence",
 "The exception covers ammunition bought and discharged at the accredited range. Buying there does not exempt unused ammunition taken home.")
Q("An unattended firearm is stolen from an unlocked ordinary cabinet because the owner never put it in prescribed storage. Which custody failure most directly explains the breach?",
 "Failing to lock the firearm away","Failing to lock the safe key away","Failing to report an address change","Failing to keep the licence legible",
 "Section 120(8) covers failure to lock away a firearm when it is not carried or under direct control.")
Q("A firearm is stolen after the owner leaves the safe key in an openly accessible place, though the safe meets the prescribed standard. Which custody failure is identified?",
 "Leaving the safe key unprotected","Not bolting the safe down","Storing ammunition with the firearm","Failing to lock the firearm away",
 "The Act expressly covers failure to keep the keys in safe custody. A compliant safe does not excuse careless key handling.")
Q("While carrying a firearm, an owner takes no sensible steps to guard against losing it and is dispossessed as a result. Which section 120(8) obligation is implicated?",
 "Taking reasonable precautions","Locking it away while carrying it","Reporting it within seven days","Keeping it unloaded while carried",
 "Reasonable precautions against loss or theft apply while the firearm is carried or under direct control, not only while it is stored.")
Q("A storage provider has an endorsed permission for one address but moves the firearm to a different safe without authority. Which aspect of the permission has been breached?",
 "The named place of storage","The period of the storage","The reason for the storage","The DFO's endorsement",
 "The permission is tied to the named place. Another safe elsewhere does not satisfy the location condition.")
assert len(ITEMS)==48
